**Correcting my opening line first, because it changes the analysis:** I said none of the censused classes would count a shift register. That is wrong. `docs/NAMES.md:263-264` records `LeftShiftRegister` 16442 and `RightShiftRegister` 16399 as **both deriving from `Tunnel`**, and the [LabVIEW Wiki](https://labviewwiki.org/wiki/RightShiftRegister_class) gives the ancestry as `Generic → GObject → Tunnel → RightShiftRegister`. Your traverse includes subclasses — your own log proves it, since a `Diagram` traverse returned a row whose class is `TopLevelDiagram` (`diag_c67_m3a.log:54`). So `Tunnel` 471 → 471 is a **real** negative. The observation half of your claim is stronger than you argued. The mechanism half is not.

# 1. The single strongest reason the claim is WRONG

**The claim's discriminating variable was never varied, and its premise rests on a reading that is self-evidently broken.**

The claim is "silently does nothing *when the target loop is not on the top-level diagram*." To support that you need one loop that is on the top-level diagram where the call works, and one that is not where it doesn't. You have neither. `add_shift_reg` was called on exactly one loop in this run, `#23032`. `#637` — your stated control — was only ever **read** (`:44-46`), never written, and `#637` is not on the top-level diagram either: it owns nested `Diagram #639` at traverse index 46 (`:36`).

Worse, the premise itself: `:54` says traverse index 0 is `TopLevelDiagram` uid 536 **with 0 nodes**, at position `(-17476, 102)`, in a VI with 632 Nodes and 173 Diagrams. A 632-node VI cannot have an empty top-level diagram — the outermost structure would itself be a top-level node. So the sentence "is `#23032` on the top-level diagram → False" is derived from a reader that returns False for *every object in the VI*. It is not a measurement of `#23032`; it is a broken reading being promoted to a causal mechanism. `find_node` even searched `hints = [di, TOP]` first and found nothing at index 0 before scanning on.

A hypothesis whose distinguishing variable is constant across every observation, and whose premise comes from an instrument returning an impossible value, explains nothing.

# 2. Alternative explanation of the same evidence

**Rival #1 — the edit was silently declined because the target's diagram was not fully in memory. I rate this highest.** This is not new LabVIEW physics; it is your own documented failure mode, in your own words at `tools/gscript.py:1244-1246`:

> "Wiring a target loaded only via GetVIReference is **silently declined (count unchanged, no error)**: the diagram is not fully in memory. OpenFrontPanel forces the full load. Discovered 2026-08-28 after three silent failures."

"Count unchanged, no error" is the observed signature, verbatim. And `diag_c67_m3a.py:77` puts `no g.open_panel` in the forbidden list, so the run *guaranteed* that condition for step 2.

The obvious objection — the seven `move_in`s and seven `connect_nested_v1`s *did* land (`Wire` 1907 → **1914** at `:554`) — actually sharpens this rather than killing it: those all ran **after** two `g.save(WORK)` calls, and a save forces a full load. The ordering is consistent with "not loaded during step 2, loaded from step 3 onward." That is a testable claim; the top-level-diagram claim is not.

**Rival #2 — `UID 2` never named a created object, and you cannot tell from this run.** `op()` caches one reference per op VI and states "Op VIs are non-reentrant" (`gscript.py:211`). A non-reentrant VI's indicators retain their last written value. `opaddshiftreg_labels.json` maps `uid → "UID 2"` and `error → "error out 3"`, and `build_opaddshiftreg_v0.py:237,244,249` shows `UID 2` is fed by a `GObject.UID` Property Node wired to the Invoke's return terminal — but `:255-261` puts the read error indicator on **the Invoke's `error out`, not the Property Node's**. So the one channel that would say "I could not read a UID off that reference" is never read by `add_shift_reg`. Whether a declined call leaves `UID 2` at 0 or at its previous value is exactly what this run does not distinguish.

Your own log also destroys the inference from the uid's value: uid **23522** was minted and deleted five times in a row (`:114, :139, :164, :189, :214`) before the next junk node became 23786. **This VI reuses freed uids.** So "23561 is stale" and "23561 was freshly minted and then freed" are not separable by the number 23561.

**Rival #3 — `Y Position`.** Rated third, below both of the above, but alive. Judging your (i): the wiki page for [Loop.Add Shift Register](https://labviewwiki.org/wiki/Loop_class/Add_Shift_Register_method) lists `Y Position` (U32) as **required** and states no clamping and no declining behaviour — it says only "Adds a shift register at the specified location on the frame of the loop." U32 means you cannot express a negative y, and `#23032` lives on nested `Diagram #686` whose coordinate origin you never read. Note `build_opaddshiftreg_v0.py:324` used y=120 on a *scratch* loop and passed 9/9, so 120 is known-good somewhere, which tells you nothing about `#23032`'s frame. **Cheapest reading:** `GObject.Bounds`/`Position` on `#23032`, then repeat at its own vertical midpoint.

# 3. Judging your rivals (ii) and (iii)

**(ii) Traverse-order mismatch — effectively dead, and I'd stop chasing it.** If the op had created a register on some *other* While Loop, `Tunnel` would still have gone 471 → 473, because the count is whole-VI and both register classes descend from `Tunnel`. It reads 471 at `:105` and again at `:554`. No shift register was created **anywhere in the VI**, so "it landed on the wrong loop" is refuted by the census you already have. Your planned reader is largely designed to chase this dead rival.

**(iii) The cast — least likely of the three.** `6361000` is owned by `Loop` 16405 and reached by inheritance (`docs/NAMES.md:256`; `archive/peer/2026-09-14-addshiftreg-fail1-branch-or-decline.md:158`). More to the point, you have *measured* what a wrong-class reference does through this exact op: it raises **loudly**. `archive/peer/2026-09-15-core-fail3-wrapper-used-while-seed.md:18` — a ForLoop reference into the WhileLoop seed gave "error 1055: Invoke Node in OpAddShiftReg_v0.vi". `#23032` came out of `report_all('WhileLoop')` and there was no error. Silent acceptance is not this failure's shape.

# 4. What does the method return, and is `''` proof it ran?

It returns a **`RightShiftRegister` refnum** ([wiki](https://labviewwiki.org/wiki/Loop_class/Add_Shift_Register_method)); `NAMES.md:841` records the Invoke's terminals as `reference`, `reference out`, `error in`, `error out`, plus `Add Shift Register` and `Y Position` **twice each**, with `build_opaddshiftreg_v0.py:218-219` measuring 4 = return sink / 5 = return source.

**No — `''` is not proof the method ran.** It is proof of one narrower thing: the indicator named `error out 3`, wired to the **Invoke node's** error out, read no-error at the moment Python sampled it. That leaves three gaps: the UID Property Node's own error chain is unread; the op VI is non-reentrant so every indicator can carry a prior run's value; and `_err` returns `None` on a `GetControlValue` exception (`gscript.py:445-446`), which `add_shift_reg` treats as falsy — an unreadable error indicator is indistinguishable from no error.

# 5. What would FALSIFY the claim

Call `add_shift_reg` on a loop that genuinely **is** on the top-level diagram (established by a reader that does not report the top-level diagram as empty) and observe `Tunnel` unchanged with a uid returned and no error. Conversely the claim earns its first real support only if that same call takes `Tunnel` 471 → 473 while the identical call on `#23032` leaves it at 471. **This run did neither.**

# 6. Cheapest discriminating test

One scratch copy of the bed, four op runs, `Tunnel` read after each. ~2 minutes, no build, no new verb:

| step | action | reading | meaning |
|---|---|---|---|
| a | `add_shift_reg(#23032, y=120)` | expect 471 | reproduce the failure |
| b | `g.open_panel(WORK)`, repeat (a) | **473 ⇒ rival #1 wins, claim dead** | the one-line fix |
| c | if still 471: read `#23032` bounds, repeat at its vertical midpoint | 473 ⇒ rival #3 | y was outside the frame |
| d | if still 471: `add_shift_reg(#637, …)` | 473 ⇒ the difference is the loop, not the diagram level | claim still wrong |

Only if a–d all stay 471 *and* a top-level loop succeeds does the claim survive. Step (b) alone is worth more than the entire five-leg census.

# 7. What your planned reader will show — and the wrong conclusions to guard against

It is a pure reader on five fresh duplicates of an unchanged bed. **It never calls `add_shift_reg`, so it cannot discriminate between any of the causes above.** Concretely:

- `minted` and `vanished` will be **empty** on all five legs; the four class counts identical; `ExecState` 1 throughout. ⚠️ Do not read "five clean legs" as evidence about `Add Shift Register`. Five copies of the same number is one measurement, not five.
- The per-loop register census will show `#637` answering at 3 slots and every other loop, `#23032` included, at 0. ⚠️ That is the bed's pre-existing state, not a finding. Concluding "confirmed: the register was never created" from it is circular — nothing in the run creates one.
- **The one genuinely useful product:** it will establish that 1055-at-every-slot is simply what an *empty* `Shift Registers[]` looks like through `OpShiftRegs_v0`, on every register-free loop. Worth having, because the current run has **no out-of-range control** — `m3a2_left_sr_feed` broke out at reg_index 2 once both targets were found (`diag_c67_m3a.py:690`), so you have never seen what "index past the end" looks like on a loop you *know* has registers. Until you do, error 1055 does not distinguish "array empty" from "loop reference invalid" from "the op's downcast failed."
- ⚠️ An empty uid-set diff will tempt "uids are stable." Your own log says otherwise (`:114–:214`, uid 23522 reused five times).

